'''Christchurch Central''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] in the [[South Island]] city of [[Christchurch]]. The electorate was established for the [[1946 New Zealand general election|1946 election]] and, until [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011]] had always been won by the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. Since 2008, the incumbent was [[Brendon Burns (politician)|Brendon Burns]] but the election night results for the {{NZ election link|2011}} resulted in a tie; the special vote results combined with a judicial recount revealed a 47-vote majority for [[Nicky Wagner]], the National list MP based in the electorate. Wagner significantly increased her winning margin in the {{NZ election link|2014}} after having declared the electorate "unwinnable" for National earlier in the year following a boundary review. At the {{NZ election link|2017}}, Wagner lost the seat to Labour's [[Duncan Webb]], who has retained it since the {{NZ election link|2020}}.

==Population centres==
The [[1941 New Zealand census]] had been postponed due to World War II, so the 1946 electoral redistribution had to take ten years of population growth and movements into account. The [[North Island]] gained a further two electorates from the [[South Island]] due to faster population growth. The abolition of the [[country quota]] through the ''Electoral Amendment Act, 1945'' reduced the number and increased the size of rural electorates. None of the existing electorates remained unchanged, 27 electorates were abolished, eight former electorates were re-established, and 19 electorates were created for the first time, including Christchurch Central.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=91–96}}

As the name suggests, the electorate covers the [[Christchurch Central City]], plus several inner suburbs to the north and east of the central city. Since the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]], the following suburbs, in alphabetical order, are at least partially located in the electorate: [[Avonside]], Central City, [[Edgeware]], [[Linwood, New Zealand|Linwood]], [[Mairehau]], [[Merivale, Christchurch|Merivale]], [[Northcote, Christchurch|Northcote]], [[Papanui]], [[Phillipstown, New Zealand|Phillipstown]], [[Redwood, Canterbury|Redwood]], [[Richmond, Canterbury|Richmond]], [[Shirley, New Zealand|Shirley]], [[St Albans, New Zealand|St Albans]], [[Sydenham, New Zealand|Sydenham]], and [[Waltham, New Zealand|Waltham]].<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/mapping/ |title= Electorate Boundaries |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date= 24 November 2011 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130208035645/http://www.elections.org.nz/mapping/ |archive-date= 8 February 2013 |url-status= dead }}</ref> In the 2013/14 redistribution, the electorate lost Mairehau and Shirley to Christchurch East and gained more of Sydenham and [[Beckenham, New Zealand|Beckenham]] from [[Port Hills (New Zealand electorate)|Port Hills]] and more of Redwood from [[Waimakariri (New Zealand electorate)|Waimakariri]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain the [[Addington, New Zealand|Addington]]-[[Spreydon]] suburban area from [[Wigram (New Zealand electorate)|Wigram]] while transferring parts of [[Linwood, New Zealand|Linwood]]-[[Phillipstown, New Zealand|Phillipstown]] and [[Northcote, Christchurch|Northcote]] to [[Christchurch East]] and [[Ilam (New Zealand electorate)|Ilam]] respectively.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=3 September 2025 |page=8 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
The Christchurch Central electorate was created in [[1946 New Zealand general election|1946]].<ref name="Scholefield 1950">{{cite book |last= Scholefield |first= Guy |title= New Zealand Parliamentary Record, 1840–1949 |author-link=Guy Scholefield |edition=3rd |orig-year= First ed. published 1913 |year= 1950 |publisher=Govt. Printer |location= Wellington |page=156}}</ref> [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] held the seat for the next 65 years, though a high turnout for the [[Alliance (New Zealand political party)|Alliance]] saw [[Tim Barnett (politician)|Tim Barnett]]'s [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]] majority come in at under a thousand. The incumbent, Brendon Burns, had a majority in the 2008 election of also just under one thousand.<ref>[http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/2/5/c/49MP127001-Burns-Brendon.htm New Zealand Parliament — Brendon Burns MP]</ref>

The first representative was [[Robert Macfarlane (New Zealand politician)|Robert Macfarlane]], who had earlier represented the [[Christchurch South]] electorate. He held Christchurch Central until the {{NZ election link|1969}}, when he retired. He was succeeded by [[Bruce Barclay]], who died in office in 1979. This caused the {{By-election link|Christchurch Central|1979}} held on 18 August, which was won by [[Geoffrey Palmer (New Zealand politician)|Geoffrey Palmer]]. Palmer eventually went on to become [[Prime Minister of New Zealand|Prime Minister]].<ref name="Palmer short bio">{{cite web|title=Geoffrey Palmer|url=https://nzhistory.govt.nz/people/geoffrey-palmer|publisher=Ministry for Culture and Heritage|access-date=19 November 2011|date=9 July 2010}}</ref>

Palmer retired at the {{NZ election link|1990}} and was succeeded by [[Lianne Dalziel]]. At the {{NZ election link|1996}}, i.e. with the advent of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|MMP]], Dalziel did not contest an electorate but stood as a list candidate only.  [[Tim Barnett (politician)|Tim Barnett]] succeeded her and held the electorate until the {{NZ election link|2008}}, when he retired. [[Brendon Burns (politician)|Brendon Burns]] succeeded Barnett.

The election night results for the {{NZ election link|2011}} resulted in a tie; Burns and [[Nicky Wagner]] of the National Party received 10,493 votes each. The outcome of the election thus depended on the special votes.<ref name="dead heat">{{cite news |title=Unprecedented dead heat in ChCh central |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/election-2011/6041292/Unprecedented-dead-heat-in-ChCh-central |access-date=26 November 2011 |work=[[The Press]] |date=26 November 2011}}</ref> This was the first time a tie result had been achieved since 1928.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://tvnz.co.nz/election-2011/nail-biting-dead-heat-in-christchurch-4573847|title=Nail-biting dead heat in Christchurch|date=27 November 2011|access-date=13 August 2013}}</ref> When the final vote count was announced on 10 December, Wagner was declared the winner with a majority of 45 over Burns, making the result the second-smallest majority after {{NZ electorate link|Waitakere}}. Due to the closeness of the results a judicial recount was held where Wagner's majority increased by 2 votes to 47.<ref>{{cite news |title=Election results to declare new Govt |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10772192 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=10 December 2011 |access-date=12 December 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/6137499/Recount-confirms-Christchurch-central-seat |title=Recount confirms Christchurch central seat |first=Kate |last=Chapman |date=14 December 2011 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=21 September 2014}}</ref>

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Christchurch Central.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
When draft electoral boundary changes were released, Wagner declared the electorate "unwinnable" for National. Although she was expected to not contest the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 general election]], she announced at the end of January 2014 that she would try to defend her seat.<ref>{{cite news |last=Conway |first=Glenn |title=Wagner to defend Chch Central seat |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/9667063/Wagner-to-defend-Chch-Central-seat |access-date=8 March 2014 |work=[[The Press]] |date=30 January 2014}}</ref> Labour chose Tony Milne as their candidate for Christchurch Central.<ref>{{cite news |last=Robinson |first=Shelley |title=Tony Milne to run for Chch Central |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/city-centre/9806538/Tony-Milne-to-run-for-Chch-Central |access-date=8 March 2014 |work=[[The Press]] |date=8 March 2014}}</ref> Wagner had a 2,420 majority over Milne.<ref name="Canterbury decides">{{cite news |last1=Mathewson |first1=Nicole |last2=Stylianou |first2=Georgina |last3=Fulton |first3=Tim |title=Election 2014: Canterbury decides |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/10524525/Election-2014-Canterbury-decides |access-date=21 September 2014 |work=[[The Press]] |date=21 September 2014}}</ref> The Labour Party chose Duncan Webb as its candidate for the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]]. He is a prominent lawyer and earthquake claims advocate.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stylianou |first1=Georgina |title=Claims advocate to seek election<!-- online title is different: "Christchurch lawyer Duncan Webb seeking Labour Party nomination" --> |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/80967343/lawyer-duncan-webb-confirmed-as-labours-christchurch-central-candidate |access-date=4 May 2016 |work=[[The Press]] |date=4 May 2016 |page=A7}}</ref> Webb narrowly won the seat in 2017, and massively increased his majority at the {{NZ election link|2020}} amid that year's Labour landslide.

===Members of Parliament===
Christchurch Central has been represented by eight MPs. Since its creation in 1946 until the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 general election]] it had been a safe seat for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. It was then held by the National Party until the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]] when it swung back to Labour.

